Q: Is there a more succinct way to convert a String to a Set of Characters in Java? A: <p>Your question is tricky, because you say:</p>
<p>&quot;Is there a more concise way to convert a string of text into a character set in Java?&quot;.</p>
<p>But then you add:</p>
<p>&quot;Appendix: The goal of this set is to initialize a static set so that you can write something like this:</p>
<pre><code>boolean isValidCharacter( char c) { 
   return hexSet.contains( c); 
}
</code></pre>
<p>&quot;</p>
<p>This &quot;trap&quot; has conditioned the answers, which have been aimed at answering the title of the that, when in reality what you have to answer is: &quot;That's a 'tortuous' path, don't use it&quot;.</p>
<p>Here are two methods for checking whether a character is a valid hexadecimal value and whether a string consists of characters that are valid hexadecimal values.</p>
<pre><code>boolean isHexa( char aux ) {
   if( aux &lt; 48 || aux &gt; 57 &amp;&amp; aux &lt; 65 || aux &gt; 70 &amp;&amp; aux &lt; 97 || aux &gt; 102  ) return false;  
   return true;      
}

boolean isHexa( String number ) {
   for( int i = 0; i &lt; number.length(); i ++ ) {
      int aux = number.codePointAt( i ) ;
      if( aux &lt; 48 || aux &gt; 57 &amp;&amp; aux &lt; 65 || aux &gt; 70 &amp;&amp; aux &lt; 97 || aux &gt; 102  ) return false;         
   }
   return true;      
}
</code></pre>
<p>P.S.: I'm sure the difference in performance between the various approaches goes completely unnoticed in the vast majority of scenarios, but I can't help thinking that we should use the best possible implementation, even if it doesn't affect the final result... it's probably just a quirk of mine.</p>

Q: Terminate playwright test on test fail A: <p>If I understood you correctly, you want to stop testing if you had an error.</p>
<p>In this case, try this:</p>
<pre><code>import { defineConfig } from '@playwright/test';

export default defineConfig({
  maxFailures: 1,
});
</code></pre>
<p>And don't use <code>process.exit(1);</code> in your test, it breaks the sensitive binding between the browser and your test code and is likely the cause for your Ctrl+C message. Depending on browser and operating system the effect can be different. You can remove the whole <code>try..catch</code> clause from your code, playwright likes to manage exceptions for you.</p>

Q: Zip Archive - open or extractTo is not working A: <p>I had the same problem on Windows 10. The only solution I found was just to try extractTo twice, even though the open() was successful:</p>

<pre><code>$zip = new ZipArchive;
if ($open === true) {
    $result = $zip-&gt;extractTo($destination);
    if ($result === false) {
        $result = $zip-&gt;extractTo($destination);
    }
    $zip-&gt;close();
}
</code></pre>

<p>The fact that the second extractTo() works (with no intervening action) would seem to indicate that there's nothing wrong with the archive or the destination directory.</p>

Q: When to stop using await A: <p><code>async void</code> should generally be avoided but there are cases where you might need it, in particular when you are writing an application with a windowed UI (e.g., WPF). To link up UI events like button clicks, you write an event handler and that handler is not returning a <code>Task</code>. However, if the event handler is going to perform async work like making a network request, it has to be async. The problem with <code>async void</code> is that any exceptions thrown are either lost or in some cases can terminate the application, so any <code>async void</code> event handler has to catch exceptions:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>async void OnButtonClicked()
{
    try
    {
        // Update UI to reflect that an operation is in progress...
        var result = await GetResultAsync();
        // Update UI to reflect the result of the operation...
    }
    catch (Exception exception)
    {
        // Update UI to reflect that something went wrong...
    }
}
</code></pre>
